Perfect 👌 — let’s break this down like an engineer building the “Gemini Co-Drawing” tool from scratch.  

---

# 🛠 How to Build a “Co-Drawing” App (like in your screenshot)

The goal:  
You type `“pacman with crown on it”` → The app uses **Gemini** to generate a **simple sketch-style drawing** and renders it in a browser.

---

## 1. Core Components
You’ll need three parts:

1. **Frontend (UI)**  
   - Text input (prompt box)  
   - Canvas/image area (to display the sketch)  
   - Send button  

2. **Backend (API layer)**  
   - Handles requests from frontend  
   - Calls the **Gemini API** with the user’s prompt  
   - Returns the generated sketch  

3. **Gemini API (model)**  
   - Uses Gemini’s **image generation (Flash / 2.5 Flash)** to create a minimal drawing  
   - Configured to **black-and-white doodle style**  

---

## 2. Tech Stack
- **Frontend:** React (or plain HTML/JS if you want minimal setup)  
- **Backend:** Node.js (Express) or Python (Flask/FastAPI)  
- **API:** Google’s **Gemini API** (via Google AI Studio or Vertex AI)  
- **Image Rendering:** `<img>` tag for PNG output OR `<canvas>` if you want to draw progressively  

---

## 3. Gemini API Setup
- Sign up at [Google AI Studio](https://aistudio.google.com/)  
- Get an **API Key**  
- Install client SDK:
  - For Node.js:  
    ```bash
    npm install @google/generative-ai
    ```
  - For Python:  
    ```bash
    pip install google-generativeai
    ```

---

## 4. Backend Example (Node.js + Express)
Here’s a super simple backend that takes a prompt and gets an image from Gemini:

```js
import express from "express";
import fetch from "node-fetch";

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

const API_KEY = process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY;

app.post("/generate", async (req, res) => {
  const { prompt } = req.body;

  const response = await fetch(
    "https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/models/gemini-2.5-flash:generateContent?key=" + API_KEY,
    {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      body: JSON.stringify({
        contents: [{ parts: [{ text: prompt }] }],
        generationConfig: {
          // Force doodle style
          responseMimeType: "image/png"
        }
      }),
    }
  );

  const data = await response.json();
  res.json(data);
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log("Backend running on port 3000"));
```

---

## 5. Frontend Example (React)
```jsx
import { useState } from "react";

export default function App() {
  const [prompt, setPrompt] = useState("");
  const [image, setImage] = useState("");

  const generateImage = async () => {
    const res = await fetch("/generate", {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      body: JSON.stringify({ prompt }),
    });
    const data = await res.json();
    setImage("data:image/png;base64," + data.candidates[0].content.parts[0].inlineData.data);
  };

  return (
    <div className="p-4">
      <h1 className="text-xl font-bold">Gemini Co-Drawing</h1>
      <input
        className="border p-2 w-full"
        value={prompt}
        onChange={(e) => setPrompt(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Describe your sketch..."
      />
      <button onClick={generateImage} className="mt-2 bg-black text-white px-4 py-2 rounded">
        Draw
      </button>
      {image && <img src={image} alt="Generated sketch" className="mt-4 border rounded" />}
    </div>
  );
}
```

---

## 6. Forcing “Sketch / Doodle Style”
When sending the request to Gemini, add style instructions inside the prompt, e.g.:

> “Minimal black-and-white doodle style, like a child’s sketch. Only simple line art, no shading, no colors. Inside a square frame.”

This is how the original demo produced your **Pac-Man with crown** look.  

---

## 7. Deployment
- **Local:** Run backend on `localhost:3000`, frontend on `localhost:5173` (or React default)  
- **Cloud:** Deploy backend to Vercel/Render/Cloud Run, frontend to Netlify/Vercel  

---

✅ Result: You’ll have your own **Gemini Co-Drawing clone** where you type prompts and get doodle-style sketches.

